From 7a206a32a3da6145fead129c67616603b8f9407b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 23:38:59 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] nvkms: HDR to a display that declares HDMI 3D, by what the
 head sends

NVKMS refused HDR to any display whose HDMI VSDB says 3D_present,
whether anything 3D was being sent or not. nvDpyIsHDRCapable() said no,
so every flip and mode set with HDR metadata or BT.2100 colorimetry was
refused on every GPU, Blackwell included; and on Turing, Ampere and Ada
SendHDRInfoFrame() left the HDR InfoFrame out besides. An RTX 20 to 50
sent no HDR10 at all to a TV that declares 3D: of 590 EDIDs at hand, 12
that take HDR declare 3D, eight of them LG TVs of 2016.

The reason was the slots of the SOR. On the HALs without generic shared
InfoFrames (C5, C6 and C7: Turing, Ampere, Ada), nvEvo1SendHdmiInfoFrame()
sends the HDR InfoFrame through the vendor-specific slot, the one of the
vendor InfoFrame of HDMI 3D. The generic InfoFrame slot is the other one:
the EMP of HDMI VRR holds it while HDMI VRR is on, RM keeps it on GA10x
when it reports NV0073_CTRL_SYSTEM_CAPS_HDMI21_SW_ACR_BUG_3275257, and
nvidia-open-kernel-modules-9020 puts the HDR10+ InfoFrame there; nothing
else uses it (no ALLM, no other InfoFrame). AD10x has shared generic slot
2 too, where 9020 puts HDR10+ when the EMP holds the generic one.

Now what the head sends decides (nvHadesHdmiSlots() in nvkms-hades.h,
on plain values), by its vendor InfoFrame:
 - the client's of Dolby Vision (OUI 00-D0-46, Standard or Low-Latency):
   as before, alone in the vendor-specific slot, no HDR InfoFrame, and a
   flip with HDR refused;
 - another client's (the HDMI 1.4 one of 3D side by side or top and
   bottom) and NVKMS's own of a frame-packed mode: as before, in the
   vendor-specific slot, with no SDR HDR InfoFrame over it; with HDR (the
   HDR InfoFrame enabled, not the SDR one of a transition) the HDR
   InfoFrame takes the vendor-specific slot and the vendor one goes in the
   generic slot (nvHadesEvo1SendHdmiGenericInfoFrame(), every frame, with
   its checksum), unless the EMP or RM holds it: then the 3D one keeps its
   slot, no HDR InfoFrame goes out and the kernel log says so, and a flip
   with HDR and a client's one is refused as before;
 - NVKMS's own of a 2D mode (one named by an HDMI_VIC: 4K at 24, 25 or
   30 Hz from the HDMI VSDB of a display without UHD_VIC), and none: the
   HDR InfoFrame in the vendor-specific slot, over the vendor one, as NVKMS
   sends it to every display that does not declare 3D.
An HDR10+ InfoFrame keeps the slot of 9020 unless the 3D InfoFrame takes
the generic one: then shared generic slot 2 on AD10x, and none on Turing
and Ampere, where a flip with HDR10+ next to HDR in a frame-packed mode is
refused with a line that says why. HDR10 is never left out for HDR10+.
The generic slot that the 3D InfoFrame holds is not turned off by the
HDR10+ InfoFrame leaving it, as with the EMP; when the 3D one leaves it,
it is turned off unless the EMP or HDR10+ took it over.

To a display that does not declare HDMI 3D nothing changes: the same
slots, the same packets, in the same order, on every GPU, for SDR, HDR10,
HDR10+, Dolby Vision and modes with an HDMI_VIC; the one thing added is
that a flip may carry HDR next to a client's vendor InfoFrame other than
Dolby's where the generic slot is free, which was refused. On the HALs
with generic shared InfoFrames only nvDpyIsHDRCapable() changes.

Host test (on the functions of this tree, against the tree before it):
nvUpdateHdmiInfoFrames() and nvHadesUpdateHdmiHdr10Plus() of
nvkms-hdmi.c, nvEvo1SendHdmiInfoFrame() and nvEvo1DisableHdmiInfoFrame()
of nvkms-evo1.c, nvDpyIsHDRCapable(), ValidateHdmiVsif() of
nvkms-hw-flip.c and NVIDIA's timing library unchanged, with four real
EDIDs (the LG C5, a Denon receiver with HDMI_VIC modes and no 3D, an LG
TV of 2016 and a Denon receiver that declare 3D), on Turing, Ampere with
and without the ACR workaround, Ada and Blackwell, with and without the
EMP. Every packet write and control, RM call and log line is recorded.

On hardware, with an RTX 20, 30 or 40 and a TV that declares 3D: HDR10
plays as HDR (it did not), and the kernel log has
"Hades: head N sends the HDR InfoFrame in the vendor-specific slot, and
the 3D vendor InfoFrame of the frame-packed mode in the generic InfoFrame
slot" when HDR goes with frame-packed 3D, or "Hades: head N sends no HDR
InfoFrame: ..." where the generic slot is held.
